---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Nav from "../../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import CtaBanner from "../../components/CtaBanner.astro";
import GuideList from "../../components/GuideList.astro";
import StickyCta from "../../components/StickyCta.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Local Market Research | SEO Brothers"
  description="We rank local businesses across the US and Canada. These are research deep-dives on more than fifty metros, showing how we read a market before a campaign."
>
  <Nav />
  <GuideList
    category="cities"
    label="Local Market Research"
    blurb="We rank local businesses across the US and Canada, and anywhere local search matters. These are research deep-dives on more than fifty metros, examples of how we read a market before we touch a campaign, not the limit of where we work."
    accent="neutral"
  >
    <h2>How we read a local market</h2>
    <p>
      A keyword tool will tell you that "plumber" gets searched a certain number
      of times a month in a city. It won't tell you that half those searches go
      to the map pack, that locals abbreviate the city name, that one suburb
      searches differently than the core, or that an old neighborhood name still
      pulls volume. Local search is full of that texture, and it's where
      campaigns are won or lost.
    </p>
    <p>
      So before we target a market, we research how people there actually search.
      We pull real volume for the service-plus-location combinations that matter,
      map the <a href="/guides/searcher-intent/">intent</a> behind them, and look
      at who already owns the map pack and the first page. Then we build the plan
      around the gaps. The <a href="/guides/local-seo/">local SEO guide</a> covers
      the mechanics; these market write-ups cover the local quirks.
    </p>

    <h2>The markets we've written up</h2>
    <p>
      We run campaigns across the US and Canada, and the approach below applies
      to any market. The library covers more than fifty metros and keeps
      growing. On the Canadian side,
      <a href="/guides/halifax-seo/">Halifax</a>,
      <a href="/guides/hamilton-seo/">Hamilton</a>,
      <a href="/guides/kitchener-waterloo-seo/">Kitchener-Waterloo</a>, and
      <a href="/guides/vancouver-seo/">Vancouver</a> are home markets we speak
      to from firsthand experience, with
      <a href="/guides/toronto-seo/">Toronto</a>,
      <a href="/guides/montreal-seo/">Montreal</a>,
      <a href="/guides/calgary-seo/">Calgary</a>, and
      <a href="/guides/ottawa-seo/">Ottawa</a> rounding out the map. The US
      write-ups span the country: Sun Belt metros like
      <a href="/guides/atlanta-seo/">Atlanta</a>,
      <a href="/guides/dallas-seo/">Dallas</a>,
      <a href="/guides/houston-seo/">Houston</a>, and
      <a href="/guides/phoenix-seo/">Phoenix</a>, the West Coast from
      <a href="/guides/seattle-seo/">Seattle</a> down to
      <a href="/guides/sacramento-seo/">Sacramento</a>, and the Midwest and
      Northeast from <a href="/guides/kansas-city-seo/">Kansas City</a> to
      <a href="/guides/pittsburgh-seo/">Pittsburgh</a>. The same method is what
      we apply to the <a href="/guides/industry/">industries we serve</a>
      wherever a business is trying to get found, whatever city it's in.
    </p>
  </GuideList>
  <CtaBanner />
  <StickyCta label="Get a Free SEO Audit" href="/tools/free-seo-audit/" />
  <Footer />
</Layout>
